],"w":{"pay":{"o":[{"chain":["Persian","Ottoman Turkish","Russian","Middle Chinese"],"kind":"borrowed","pos":"noun","text":"According to Nişanyan, from Persian پای (pây, “foot”), with the sense ”share” originating from the Persian expression borrowed into Old Anatolian Turkish بای برابر (pây-berâber, “equally, to the same proportion”, literally “equal foot”). The word is present in its modern sense in XIVth century Book of Dede Korkut.\nThe non-Oghuz Turkic cognates, such as Kirgiz and Yakut пай (pay, “share”) are, according to Nişanyan, a borrowing from the Ottoman Turkish پای, via Russian пай (paj).\nHowever it is more possibly borrowed from Middle Chinese 派 (pʰaiH) as early as 7th century and inherited by later Turkic languages."}]},"tank":{"o":[{"chain":["English","Portuguese"],"kind":"derived","pos":"noun","text":"Internationalism. From English tank, from Portuguese tanque, from Gujarati ટાંકી (ṭā̃kī)."}]},"arıçı":{"o":[{"chain":[],"kind":"formed","pos":"noun","text":"From arı + -çı."}]},"müxtəlifləşmək":{"o":[{"chain":[],"kind":"formed","pos":"verb","text":"From müxtəlif + -ləş."}]},"ağzıbütünlük":{"s":["soft","rule"]},"azadpərvərlik":{"s":["soft","rule"]},"qalabəndlik":{"s":["soft","rule"]},"qiyməbalıq":{"s":["soft","rule"]},"məhəlliçilik":{"s":["soft","rule"]},"məmurluq":{"s":["soft","corpus"]},"pulpərəstlik":{"s":["soft","rule"]},"tənəkəçilik":{"s":["soft","rule"]},"türksevərlik":{"s":["soft","rule"]}}}